    title: "Israel election turnout highest in 50 years as voting continues",
    summary: "Almost 45 percent of Israeli voters had cast their ballots by mid-afternoon.",
    preview: "Almost 45 percent of Israelis had cast a ballot by mid-afternoon, but questions have been raised about possible voter suppression in Arab towns. Almost 45 percent of Israeli voters had cast a ballot by mid-afternoon on Tuesday, according to Israel’s Central Elections Committee, marking the highest election turnout by 2pm (12:00 GMT) in 50 years. The figure was 6 percent higher than at the same time in the last elections in 2022.",
    body: "Almost 45 percent of Israelis had cast a ballot by mid-afternoon, but questions have been raised about possible voter suppression in Arab towns.\n\nAlmost 45 percent of Israeli voters had cast a ballot by mid-afternoon on Tuesday, according to Israel’s Central Elections Committee, marking the highest election turnout by 2pm (12:00 GMT) in 50 years.\n\nThe figure was 6 percent higher than at the same time in the last elections in 2022.\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 4 items\n\n- list 1 of 4Palestinian citizens of Israel fear they’re next target if far-right wins\n\n- list 2 of 4The Netherlands pulls out of Eurovision 2027 due to Israel ‘sensitivities’\n\n- list 3 of 4Palestinian citizens of Israel can vote, but cannot expect change\n\n- list 4 of 4Israel election results 2026: When will we know who won and what’s next?\n\nMitchell Barak, a pollster and former aide to Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, told Al Jazeera that voter turnout “is really high and that’s good”.\n\n“I think … it will probably pass its historic peak in ‘73 – when Israel was also involved in a huge war – so draw what conclusion from that you will,” he added.\n\nThe election is largely seen as a referendum on Netanyahu’s handling of the October 7, 2023 Hamas-led attacks and the wars that followed, the most intense period of combat for Israel since the 1973 surprise military campaign by Egypt and Syria.\n\n“Those numbers are going to help the incumbent parties,” Barak said, “as the 3.25 percent threshold a party needs to get into the Knesset remains, the number of votes you need to meet that threshold increases dramatically.”\n\nIsraeli officials noted that several polling stations had faced exceptionally long lines, which were attributed to high voter turnout despite rainy and blustery weather in parts of the country, and a flood warning in some areas.\n\nArab vote\n\nIn a message more than five hours after polls opened, Netanyahu urged supporters to vote, saying turnout was high among his opponents on the left and among Palestinian citizens of Israel, about a fifth of the population.\n\n“We need it to be high too. You all need to stream to the polls,” he said. “This is a fateful moment.”\n\nNetanyahu had repeatedly warned supporters of the prospect of high turnout among Palestinian citizens in Israel ahead of the vote, seeking to mobilise his base under the slogan “the right or Palestine”.\n\nThose voters were casting ballots in “unprecedented” numbers, Palestinian candidate Ahmed Tibi said.\n\nNetanyahu and his main rival Gadi Eisenkot have ruled out partnering with Arab parties, citing a lack of shared values, although some possible scenarios envision them propping up a minority government to ensure Netanyahu is removed from office.\n\nMeanwhile, an Israeli rights group has filed a complaint with the country’s election commission warning that widespread delays could be suppressing the vote among Israeli Palestinians.\n\nThough delays could be a result of high voter turnout, the Association for Civil Rights in Israel (ACRI) said in its complaint to the Central Elections Committee that delays had been reported in at least 18 communities. It asked the committee to investigate “whether these delays are deliberate.”\n\nThe ACRI also reported “a large presence” of police and National Guard officers at a polling station in the Negev Desert, raising concerns that officers were using crowd control as a pretext to prevent people from entering to vote.",
